= i
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**Symmetric wetting means that both film boundaries prefer the same block**: A at both, or B at both. **Asymmetric wetting means opposite preferences**, such as A at the substrate and B at the free surface. A **neutral boundary** has approximately equal interfacial free energies against A and B and therefore imposes no first-order preference between them.

These terms compare the substrate–polymer and atmosphere–polymer interfaces, not the volume fractions or molecular weights of the two blocks. A film can have one neutral boundary and one selective boundary; saying only that its top surface is neutral does not make both interfaces neutral. Surface preference competes with chain stretching and the energetic cost of A/B contacts in selecting a <block copolymer> morphology.

= ii
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Symmetric wetting and an A-preferring substrate imply an **A-preferring free surface as well**. Parallel lamellae satisfy both preferences. For equal-width A and B domains, a thickness $2L_0$ contains two repeats, with terminal A half-domains:
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{A}_{L_0/4}\mid\mathrm{B}_{L_0/2}\mid\mathrm{A}_{L_0/2}\mid\mathrm{B}_{L_0/2}\mid\mathrm{A}_{L_0/4}.}
$$
The sequence is written from substrate to atmosphere. Its total thickness is $2L_0$, the total A and B thicknesses are each $L_0$, and both interfaces contact A. A diagram using five full half-period domains would instead give $2.5L_0$ and would not match the stated thickness.

The quarter-period end domains place an A-domain centre at each boundary in this ideal symmetric-lamella model. Real surface reconstruction and non-equal block fractions can change their detailed widths without changing the key requirement of identical terminations.

\Image[paper-2-lamellar-wetting.png]
{title=Lamellar terminations, thickness terraces and a single neutral boundary}
{description=Original idealized equal-block diagrams. The neutral-top panel is one admissible mixed arrangement, not a unique prediction; the bottom surface still prefers A.}

= iii
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

An asymmetric film with a B-preferring substrate must have an **A-preferring atmosphere interface**. A parallel stack with half-integer thickness $2.5L_0$ can terminate correctly at both surfaces. For equal block widths, one explicit sequence from bottom to top is
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{B}_{L_0/4}\mid\mathrm{A}_{L_0/2}\mid\mathrm{B}_{L_0/2}\mid\mathrm{A}_{L_0/2}\mid\mathrm{B}_{L_0/2}\mid\mathrm{A}_{L_0/4}.}
$$
The thicknesses add to $2.5L_0$, with $1.25L_0$ of each block. B contacts the substrate and A contacts the atmosphere, as shown in the accompanying schematic. The alternating widths in the interior reflect half-period domains, while the terminal domains are half of an interior domain in this idealized construction.

= iv
{parent=18}
{scope}

= a
{parent=iv}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

With identical A-selective boundaries, stress-free parallel stacks favour approximately integer multiples of $L_0$. An arbitrary imposed thickness generally cannot preserve both the bulk period and those terminations everywhere.

**A small mismatch can be accommodated by compressing or stretching the lamellae.** A larger mismatch can favour terraces, islands or holes with locally commensurate heights, separated by steps of roughly $L_0$. For example, a mean height $\overline h=2.35L_0$ can be assembled from regions of heights $2L_0$ and $3L_0$, with area fraction $f=0.35$ at the larger height:
$$
\overline h=(1-f)(2L_0)+f(3L_0).
$$
The cartoon shows this volume-conserving alternative. Which response wins depends on elastic stretching energy, surface energies, lateral constraints, thickness mismatch and equilibration time. If the substrate and processing permit it, dewetting or a different domain orientation can also occur; an arbitrary thickness does not prove that a single specific terrace pattern must form.

= b
{parent=iv}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

**Neutralizing the top boundary removes its preference for A, but the substrate still prefers A.** The original integer-period constraint from two A-selective interfaces is therefore relaxed. The film can adjust its top termination without paying the same surface penalty, and thickness frustration can be reduced.

A possible morphology is an A-rich wetting layer near the substrate with lamellae turning towards a more perpendicular arrangement in the upper film, as schematically illustrated. A predominantly parallel stack is also possible if the substrate anchoring and thickness commensurability dominate. Neutrality of the top alone does not uniquely determine the orientation.

If **both** boundaries were neutral, perpendicular lamellae could expose comparable A and B areas without a selective-surface penalty and would avoid the same parallel-stack thickness constraint. That is a different boundary condition from the one given here. A correct sketch must retain the A preference at the bottom rather than silently replacing it with a second neutral interface.

= 19
{parent=Section A(iii)}
{scope}

= i
{parent=19}
{scope}

= a
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

**Positive <dielectrophoresis> acts up the gradient of the squared RMS field, towards stronger-field regions; negative <dielectrophoresis> acts down that gradient, towards weaker-field regions.** The sign is the sign of $\Re\widetilde K(\omega)$.

The force is not generally tangent to an <electric field> line. Field lines show the direction of the field itself, whereas $\nabla |\mathbf E_{\rm RMS}|^2$ describes how its strength changes in space. A spatially uniform field gives zero dipole-order dielectrophoretic force even when it is large. Reversing the field each half-cycle does not reverse this time-averaged force, because the relevant intensity-like quantity is quadratic.

= b
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

At fixed material response, frequency and field-strength gradient,
$$
|\langle\mathbf F_{\rm DEP}\rangle|\propto R^3\propto V_p,
\qquad V_p=\frac{4\pi R^3}{3}.
$$
Thus **the force is proportional to particle volume**. Doubling the radius multiplies the force by eight, not two. This scaling assumes that the particle is small enough for the dipole approximation and does not substantially distort the prescribed field distribution. The mechanical drift speed need not scale with volume, because the hydrodynamic drag also changes with particle size.

= ii
{parent=19}
{scope}

= a
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For ordinary lossless dielectrics with positive real permittivities,
$$
K=\frac{\epsilon_p-\epsilon_m}{\epsilon_p+2\epsilon_m}.
$$
The denominator is positive, so
$$
\boxed{\text{positive dielectrophoresis: }\epsilon_p>\epsilon_m.}
$$
The particle is more polarizable than the displaced medium and is driven towards the region where its polarization lowers the field-plus-source energy most effectively. If the two permittivities match, this ideal dipole contrast vanishes.

= b
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Under the same positive, real-permittivity assumptions,
$$
\boxed{\text{negative dielectrophoresis: }\epsilon_p<\epsilon_m.}
$$
The particle is less polarizable than the surrounding medium and moves towards weaker-field regions. The sign describes the **polarizability contrast**, not a negative net electric charge. Conducting or lossy media require the real part of the full complex <Clausius-Mossotti factor>; a comparison of only the real dielectric constants is then insufficient.

= iii
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The narrow electrode tip concentrates field lines and produces the strong-field region. **For positive dielectrophoresis, both particles move towards that region; for negative dielectrophoresis, both move away.** Particle 1 is approximately on the central symmetry line, so its arrow is approximately horizontal. Particle 2 lies above it, so a qualitative gradient arrow points towards or away from the high-field tip rather than simply following its local field line.

The source drawing makes particle 2 approximately twice the radius of particle 1. With the explicitly stipulated equal gradient magnitudes and identical material,
$$
\boxed{\frac{|F_2|}{|F_1|}=\left(\frac{R_2}{R_1}\right)^3\simeq8.}
$$
Accordingly, the second force arrow should be much longer in either sign case. The exact ratio should remain expressed through the drawn radius ratio because the figure supplies no calibrated particle dimensions. Exact off-axis force directions would require solving the electrode boundary-value problem; the drawing supports a qualitative strong-field/weak-field distinction.

\Image[paper-2-dielectrophoresis.png]
{title=Positive and negative dielectrophoresis near a field-concentrating electrode}
{description=Original electrode and particle schematic. The large-to-small radius ratio is two, the equal-gradient force-arrow ratio is eight, and the field curves are qualitative guides rather than a numerical electrostatic solution.}

= iv
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Different materials have different frequency-dependent polarization and conduction. For an $e^{i\omega t}$ convention, a useful relative complex permittivity is $\widetilde\epsilon_r=\epsilon_r-i\sigma/(\epsilon_0\omega)$. Substituting the particle and medium responses into $\widetilde K$ determines whether each population undergoes positive or negative <dielectrophoresis> and with what magnitude.

**Choose a frequency at which one population has $\Re\widetilde K>0$ and another has $\Re\widetilde K<0$.** A nonuniform electrode field then sends them towards different regions, allowing collection in separate channels or trapping one while the other passes. Alternatively, different positive-force magnitudes can produce different deflections in a flowing suspension. A crossover where $\Re\widetilde K=0$ can selectively suppress the force on one material.

The useful frequency depends on the surrounding medium as well as the particle. An unchanging, real-permittivity model would not by itself give frequency-selective separation; dielectric dispersion or conductivity contrast is needed. Particle size affects force magnitude, and heating, electro-osmotic flow and surface polarization can complicate a real separation.

= Section B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= 20
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= i
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**An Au-containing seed absorbs gallium and forms a liquid Au–Ga alloy under the selected growth conditions.** The supplied binary diagram indicates which temperature and Ga fraction lie in the liquid field; alloying allows a liquid catalyst at temperatures far below the melting point of pure Au. Vapour precursors supply Ga and As, which enter or react through the droplet and precipitate GaAs at the liquid–solid interface when the growth chemical potentials make incorporation favourable.

The droplet remains near the wire tip as interface nucleation and step propagation extend the <nanowire>. Its footprint helps select the wire diameter, though sidewall growth can change the final profile. The catalyst is a reservoir and reaction interface, not a stoichiometric constituent consumed once per GaAs unit.

Low As concentration makes the Au–Ga diagram a useful first approximation to catalyst state, but **does not mean that arsenic supply or chemical potential is irrelevant**. A binary phase diagram alone cannot calculate supersaturation with respect to ternary GaAs formation, and nanoscale interfacial energies can shift phase boundaries. In situ composition measurements have directly distinguished substantial Ga content from the very small As content of Au-seeded catalyst droplets.<In situ analysis of catalyst composition during gold catalyzed GaAs nanowire growth by Carina B. Maliakkal and others>[2019, catalyst spectroscopy and comparison with Au–Ga–As phase calculations]{ref}

\Image[paper-2-vls-nanowire.png]
{title=VLS growth, crystallographic stacking choices and a representative faceted wire section}
{description=Original mechanism schematic and idealized cross-section. No source phase diagram is reproduced; the cross-section assumes a common $[111]$ zinc-blende growth direction and is not a unique consequence of the bulk crystal label.}

= ii
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

<Zinc blende> and <wurtzite> differ in their stacking of tetrahedrally bonded bilayers: an ideal cubic sequence is ABCABC, while the hexagonal sequence is ABAB. During <vapor–liquid–solid growth>, each new interfacial nucleus can choose between continuing the existing sequence and changing it.

**The selected phase can be the one with the smaller nucleation barrier, rather than the lower bulk free energy alone.** A nucleus at the droplet–wire–vapour triple line has edge and interfacial energies different from a nucleus fully inside the liquid–solid interface. Schematically, its free energy combines a favourable chemical-potential term with new edge/interface costs and, when appropriate, a stacking penalty. Supersaturation changes the relative importance of these terms; the droplet shape and contact angle change which nucleation sites are available.

Control is therefore possible through temperature, III/V supply ratio, precursor fluxes, catalyst composition, droplet volume/contact angle and wire diameter. Monitoring growth rather than only inspecting cooled specimens helps determine which parameter actually changes the interface. A nucleation model for Au-catalysed III–V wires explicitly showed how triple-line nucleation can favour wurtzite under suitable interface energies and supersaturation.<Why does wurtzite form in nanowires of III-V zinc blende semiconductors by Frank Glas and others>[2007, competing phase nucleation at the triple line]{ref} There is no universal rule that one phase always corresponds to the larger temperature or smaller diameter regardless of catalyst and interface geometry.

= iii
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For the common **$[111]$-axis zinc-blende GaAs wire**, a representative cross-section is a faceted hexagon with low-index sidewalls, often described by $\{110\}$-type facets. The threefold crystallographic symmetry of a polar $[111]$ direction can make alternate facets inequivalent, so triangular or truncated-triangular sections are also possible. **The zinc-blende label alone does not fix a unique hexagon**: growth direction, termination, twins, surface chemistry and kinetic facet selection also matter.

The accompanying sketch shows a representative faceted section rather than a circular cylinder. Facets that advance slowly in their normal direction tend to remain exposed, so a kinetic growth shape need not be an equilibrium Wulff shape.

A simple cause of tapering is **continued radial <vapor–solid growth> on sidewalls while the tip grows axially**. A lower wire segment has been exposed to precursors for longer than a segment formed near the end of growth. For a roughly constant radial growth speed $v_r$, its radius gains about $v_r[t_{\rm end}-t_{\rm formation}(z)]$, giving a larger base. Substrate-fed species, changing catalyst size and droplet wetting can also contribute. Tapering is not proof that the original VLS droplet was wider at every earlier stage.

= iv
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**Cooling changes the catalyst state that is being measured.** Crossing Au–Ga phase boundaries can cause solidification, segregation and intermetallic formation. Solubility changes can expel Ga or precipitate additional GaAs after the intended growth interval. The droplet contact angle and interfacial shape also change as temperature and composition change.

A cooled catalyst–wire interface can consequently have a different composition, phase assemblage, geometry and even last-grown crystal segment from the active growth interface. Continued precursor supply during cooling can add another uncontrolled history. An ex situ observation of a solid catalyst does not prove that growth occurred by a solid-catalyst mechanism, and its final geometry cannot simply be substituted into a nucleation model for the liquid growth stage.

Use in situ microscopy, diffraction or spectroscopy under representative growth conditions, or compare controlled interruption and quench protocols. Quenching reduces some rearrangement but does not guarantee a perfectly frozen interface. The Au-catalysed GaAs composition experiment provides a direct example of measuring catalyst state during growth instead of inferring it from the cooled residue.<In situ analysis of catalyst composition during gold catalyzed GaAs nanowire growth by Carina B. Maliakkal and others>[2019, motivation for and implementation of in situ catalyst analysis]{ref}

= v
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

A c-axis <wurtzite> ZnO wire has crystallographically polar basal ends, conventionally Zn-terminated $(0001)$ and O-terminated $(000\bar1)$, and approximately nonpolar prismatic side facets such as $\{10\bar10\}$. **Crystallographic polarity is not identical to net surface charge in a growth solution**: protonation, adsorbates and ionic speciation can change the latter.

For <face-selective electrostatic growth inhibition>, an oppositely charged additive adsorbs preferentially to a particular face and obstructs incorporation there. Blocking the axial end lowers axial growth relative to radial growth and yields a smaller aspect ratio; blocking sidewalls suppresses radial growth and favours long thin wires.

A primary hydrothermal experiment at pH 11 found negatively charged basal top surfaces and positively charged sidewalls. Positively charged Cd-containing complexes inhibited the top and favoured platelets; negatively charged Al-containing complexes inhibited sidewalls and favoured large aspect ratios. These signs are condition-specific, not fixed by the formal Zn/O termination alone.<Face-selective electrostatic control of hydrothermal zinc oxide nanowire synthesis by Jaebum Joo and others>[2011, Fig. 1 and pH-dependent complex-ion and surface-charge discussion]{ref}

\Image[paper-2-zno-facets.png]
{title=Facet-selective adsorption changes axial and radial ZnO growth}
{description=Original facet-termination diagram and schematic of the cited pH-11 effective surface-charge example. The signs in the adsorption panels denote solution-condition-dependent surface charge, not a universal assignment of charge to crystallographic facets.}

= 21
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= i
{parent=21}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

One consistent five-postulate formulation is the following; different textbooks group the same content differently.

* **State.** A pure state is a normalized vector $|\psi\rangle$ in a complex <Hilbert space>, with $\langle\psi|\psi\rangle=1$; vectors differing only by an overall phase represent the same state. For a spinless particle in position representation, $\psi(\mathbf r)=\langle\mathbf r|\psi\rangle$ is its wavefunction.
* **Observables.** A physical observable A is represented by a <self-adjoint operator> $\hat A$. In a Cartesian position representation, $\hat x=x$ and $\hat p_x=-i\hbar\partial_x$, with $\hbar=h/(2\pi)$.
* **Outcomes and probabilities.** Allowed ideal measurement outcomes are spectral values $a$ of $\hat A$. The <Born rule> gives $P(a)=\langle\psi|P_a|\psi\rangle$, where $P_a$ projects onto the corresponding eigenspace; continuous spectra require the analogous spectral probability measure. The expectation is $\langle\hat A\rangle=\langle\psi|\hat A|\psi\rangle$.
* **Ideal measurement update.** Conditional on a nonzero-probability outcome $a$, an ideal projective measurement leaves $P_a|\psi\rangle/\sqrt{\langle\psi|P_a|\psi\rangle}$. For a nondegenerate result this is its eigenstate. This is the ideal projection rule, not a description of every general measurement apparatus.
* **Time evolution.** Between measurements a closed system evolves by the <Schrödinger equation>, $i\hbar\partial_t|\psi(t)\rangle=\hat H|\psi(t)\rangle$, with <Hamiltonian> $\hat H$. For time-independent $\hat H$, this gives unitary evolution $e^{-i\hat Ht/\hbar}$.

The position-dependent probability density is $|\psi|^2$, and its flux is the <probability current>. These distinguish the wave amplitude from the measurable particle flow in the following scattering problem.

= ii
{parent=21}
{scope}

= a
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Place the interface at $x=0$ and let the incident beam travel in the positive x direction. Set the vacuum potential to zero and the solid potential lower by $V_0>0$:
$$
\boxed{V(x)=\begin{cases}0&x<0,\\-V_0&x\geq0.\end{cases}}
$$
Equivalently, $V(x)=-V_0\Theta(x)$. The incident kinetic energy is $E=mv^2/2$. This ideal <potential step> assumes a sharp, planar interface, constant particle mass and no absorption, inelastic processes or lateral structure. It models the prescribed decrease in potential without asserting that every real solid has the same electronic interface potential.

= b
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a stationary scattering state, the time-independent <Schrödinger equation> is $-\hbar^2\psi''/(2m)+V\psi=E\psi$. The left side contains the incident and reflected waves, while the solid has no incident wave arriving from positive infinity:
$$
\psi_<(x)=Ae^{ik_1x}+Be^{-ik_1x},\qquad
\psi_>(x)=Ce^{ik_2x},
$$
$$
k_1=\frac{\sqrt{2mE}}{\hbar},\qquad
k_2=\frac{\sqrt{2m(E+V_0)}}{\hbar}.
$$
Multiplying by $e^{-iEt/\hbar}$ gives the time-dependent wavefunctions. The kinetic energy rises inside the lower-potential solid, so $k_2>k_1$ and the transmitted wavelength is shorter. **A downward step permits a propagating transmitted wave for every $E>0$, but still generally reflects some incident flux.** There is no forbidden region merely because $E<V_0$; the relevant transmitted kinetic energy is $E+V_0$.

Classically every particle crosses a downward step and accelerates, so this partial reflection has no classical counterpart. If the step is instead upward to $U>0$, $E>U$ gives oscillatory transmission with possible reflection, while $E<U$ gives a decaying evanescent wave. A semi-infinite forbidden region carries no transmitted stationary flux; a finite barrier can permit <quantum tunnelling> to a propagating region beyond it.

= c
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a finite step and equal masses, continuity of the wavefunction and its derivative gives
$$
A+B=C,\qquad k_1(A-B)=k_2C.
$$
Hence the reflected and transmitted amplitude ratios are
$$
r=\frac BA=\frac{k_1-k_2}{k_1+k_2},\qquad
 t=\frac CA=\frac{2k_1}{k_1+k_2}.
$$
A travelling wave has <probability current> $j=(\hbar k/m)|A|^2$, so the flux coefficients are
$$
\boxed{R=|r|^2=\left(\frac{k_1-k_2}{k_1+k_2}\right)^2},\qquad
\boxed{T=\frac{k_2}{k_1}|t|^2=\frac{4k_1k_2}{(k_1+k_2)^2}}.
$$
They satisfy $R+T=1$. Using $|t|^2$ alone as the transmission probability would omit the different transmitted velocity.

For $E=2\,\mathrm{eV}$ and a downward step of $2\,\mathrm{eV}$, the transmitted kinetic energy is $4\,\mathrm{eV}$ and $k_2/k_1=\sqrt2$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{R=0.0294\;(2.94\%),\qquad T=0.9706\;(97.1\%).}
$$
The nonzero reflection is compatible with current conservation even though the potential drops.

= d
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Here the step is upward by $U=3\,\mathrm{eV}$, so the forbidden-region energy difference is $U-E=1\,\mathrm{eV}$. The bounded wavefunction is
$$
\psi_>(x)=Ce^{-\kappa x},\qquad
\kappa=\frac{\sqrt{2m(U-E)}}{\hbar}.
$$
The requested **amplitude** penetration depth is $\delta=1/\kappa$. Using the supplied mass and $h$,
$$
\boxed{\delta=\frac{6.63\times10^{-34}/(2\pi)}{\sqrt{2(9.11\times10^{-31})(1.60218\times10^{-19})}}
=1.95\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm m=0.195\,\mathrm{nm}.}
$$
The probability density decays as $e^{-2\kappa x}$, so its $1/e$ depth is half this value. For an ideal lossless semi-infinite step below threshold, the evanescent tail does not imply net stationary particle current deep into the material; the reflected flux is unity.

= e
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

**<Scanning tunneling microscopy>** uses electron tunnelling between a conducting tip and a sample across a narrow vacuum gap. At fixed bias, the strong distance dependence of the current enables sensitive height or electronic-structure measurements; the result depends on local electronic states as well as topography.

**A <tunnel diode>** uses tunnelling between occupied and available states across a very thin, heavily doped semiconductor junction. The alignment of those states changes with bias and can produce a region of negative differential resistance, useful in fast switching or oscillation circuits. Both examples require a finite barrier with states available on the other side; an infinitely extended forbidden region is not an equivalent transport geometry.

